rocket-compiler for Debian and Armbian
======================================

What the package is
-------------------

One binary package, rocket-compiler (amd64, arm64):

  /usr/bin/rocket-compiler                                    Rust, dlopens:
  /usr/lib/<triplet>/rocket-compiler/libIREECompiler.so       IREE + LLVM +
                                                              the Rocket plugin
  /usr/lib/<triplet>/rocket-compiler/iree-lld                 RUNPATH $ORIGIN
  /usr/share/rocket-compiler/rocket_conv2d_transform_spec.mlir

libIREECompiler.so is a private library on purpose. Its SONAME is bare
(`libIREECompiler.so`, no version), it is a custom build carrying this
project's target plugin, and the transform spec only parses with the IREE
it was written against. So it lives in the package's own directory, not in
a public library package, and the binary, library and spec always ship and
upgrade together. It cannot clash with an upstream IREE install.

rocket-compiler finds the library and spec through absolute paths baked in
at build time: debian/rules exports ROCKET_COMPILER_LIBDIR and
ROCKET_COMPILER_DATADIR, which rocket-compiler/src/main.rs reads with
option_env!. The lookup order is --iree-compiler-lib, then
IREE_COMPILER_LIB, then those paths, then the nightly tarball's relative
layout. No environment, wrapper script or symlink is needed.

The orig tarball
----------------

debian/make-orig.sh builds rocket-compiler_<upstream>.orig.tar.xz (about
3 GB unpacked) from git objects. It contains:

  - this repository without debian/;
  - IREE at the recorded submodule commit, with only the five IREE
    submodules the compiler build reads: llvm-project, torch-mlir (without
    its nested externals/), flatcc, benchmark and googletest;
  - every crate in Cargo.lock, vendored under vendor/.

debian/rules builds with `cargo --offline` whenever vendor/ is present, so
the build works with no network, as sbuild and Armbian's builders require.

  git submodule update --init iree-build/iree-src
  git -C iree-build/iree-src submodule update --init \
      third_party/{llvm-project,torch-mlir,flatcc,benchmark,googletest}
  debian/make-orig.sh                    # writes ../rocket-compiler_*.orig.tar.xz

In a git worktree whose submodules are not initialized, point it at another
checkout with IREE_REPO=/path/to/iree-src. REV=$(git stash create) packages
uncommitted edits for a trial build.

Building
--------

A full build compiles LLVM and takes hours on a cold tree, and longer on an
arm64 board. Two shortcuts that do not change the package's contents:

  ROCKET_IREE_BUILD_DIR=/path/to/iree-build/build
      Package an existing compiler-host build (lib/libIREECompiler.so and
      tools/iree-lld) instead of building IREE. This is the nightly CI's
      build, so a .deb job can reuse it.

  ROCKET_IREE_CMAKE_ARGS="-DCMAKE_C_COMPILER_LAUNCHER=ccache ..."
      Extra CMake arguments for the IREE configure step.

  dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

The build runs the unit tests and then compiles one model
(rocket-compiler-plugin/test/rocket_conv_layout.mlir) with the library being
packaged. A spec the library cannot parse fails the build, not the user's
first compile. debian/tests/smoke repeats that against the installed package
with an empty environment, as an autopkgtest.

The Rust toolchain
------------------

The crates use edition 2024, so the build needs rustc >= 1.85. trixie has it.
bookworm, jammy and noble do not have one that new as their default rustc
(Ubuntu has versioned rustc-1.xx packages, which do not satisfy the
Build-Depends as written). On those, install Rust
with rustup and build with `dpkg-buildpackage -d` to skip the build-dependency
check.

One build per suite
-------------------

The binaries require the glibc and libstdc++ of the suite they were built
on. A build on noble (glibc 2.39) will not load on bookworm (2.36) or jammy
(2.35). Build once per target suite, in a container or chroot of that suite,
and publish each .deb to that suite's component of the apt repository.

Known lintian tags
------------------

  spelling-error-in-binary (libIREECompiler.so)   LLVM and IREE's strings;
                                                  overridden
  executable-in-usr-lib (iree-lld)                private helper; overridden
  hardening-no-fortify-functions (rocket-compiler) usual for Rust binaries
  debug-file-with-no-debug-symbols (iree-lld)     a 16 KB wrapper with
                                                  nothing to keep
  initial-upload-closes-no-bugs                   not aimed at Debian proper

